-        <div class="detail-main"><h2>Real estate investing in Little River, SC</h2><p>Little River is the Grand Strand's northern, working-waterfront edge, just below the North Carolina line. It is built around the Intracoastal Waterway, a historic fishing village, and a pair of casino cruise boats. It offers a different value proposition from the beach towns: more affordable entry, genuine waterfront, and a growing year-round population rather than a pure tourism economy.</p><p>For investors, Little River is increasingly a value and growth story. As pricing on the central and southern Strand has risen, buyers and renters have moved north. Little River's combination of lower entry points, waterway frontage, and new development has drawn primary residents, long-term tenants, and a measure of vacation demand. It rewards investors who want coastal exposure without prime-beach pricing.</p><h2>Little River property types and pricing</h2><p>Inventory includes waterway and marina-adjacent homes, newer single-family subdivisions, manufactured and modular housing, and condos. Waterfront and boat-access properties carry premiums tied to the marinas and the fishing economy; inland subdivisions offer some of the more affordable single-family entry on the Strand, which supports a long-term-rental strategy with sensible cash flow.</p><p>The growth trajectory matters. New construction and infrastructure on the north end suggest a market still maturing, which can favor early, patient investors who buy ahead of the curve.</p><h2>Where investors are looking in Little River</h2><p>Waterway frontage drives the premium niche, with Coquina Harbour and the marina district at the center of boat-access demand. Golf and master-planned communities like Heather Glen and Bridgewater add inventory for primary residents and long-term tenants. Newer subdivisions toward Highways 9 and 31 offer some of the more affordable single-family entry on the northern Strand, supporting straightforward long-term-rental math.</p><h2>The Little River rental market</h2><p>Long-term rental is the steadier core, supported by the year-round population and by workers across the northern Strand. Short-term and seasonal rental exists, especially for waterway and boat-access properties, but the market is more residential than the beach corridors. As an unincorporated part of Horry County, county rules govern most short-term rental, and Chapter3 will confirm the specifics per parcel.</p><figure style="margin:2rem 0;background:#fff;border:1px solid var(--rule);border-radius:12px;padding:1.5rem 1.25rem 1rem"><figcaption style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.72rem;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:1rem">Typical short-term rental occupancy by month</figcaption><svg viewBox="0 0 680 320" style="width:100%;height:auto" role="img" aria-label="Bar chart of typical short-term rental occupancy by month: Jan 24 percent, Feb 28 percent, Mar 38 percent, Apr 50 percent, May 58 percent, Jun 68 percent, Jul 78 percent, Aug 74 percent, Sep 54 percent, Oct 53 percent, Nov 36 percent, Dec 28 percent."><style>.bar rect{transition:fill .15s ease}.bar:hover rect{fill:#a86230}</style><line x1="56" y1="268.0" x2="664" y2="268.0" stroke="rgba(28,32,40,.08)" stroke-width="1"/><text x="48" y="271.0" font-family="DM Sans,sans-serif" font-size="10" fill="#8a8276" text-anchor="end">0%</text><line x1="56" y1="207.0" x2="664" y2="207.0" stroke="rgba(28,32,40,.08)" stroke-width="1"/><text x="48" y="210.0" font-family="DM Sans,sans-serif" font-size="10" fill="#8a8276" text-anchor="end">25%</text><line x1="56" y1="146.0" x2="664" y2="146.0" stroke="rgba(28,32,40,.08)" stroke-width="1"/><text x="48" y="149.0" font-family="DM Sans,sans-serif" font-size="10" fill="#8a8276" text-anchor="end">50%</text><line x1="56" y1="85.0" x2="664" y2="85.0" stroke="rgba(28,32,40,.08)" stroke-width="1"/><text x="48" y="88.0" font-family="DM Sans,sans-serif" font-size="10"