 text-anchor="middle">Oct</text></g><g class="bar"><rect x="572.3" y="180.2" width="31.4" height="87.8" rx="3" fill="var(--brass)"><title>Nov: 36% occupancy</title></rect><text x="588.0" y="175.2" font-family="DM Sans,sans-serif" font-size="10" fill="#5a5346" text-anchor="middle">36%</text><text x="588.0" y="284.0" font-family="DM Sans,sans-serif" font-size="10" fill="#8a8276" text-anchor="middle">Nov</text></g><g class="bar"><rect x="622.9" y="199.7" width="31.4" height="68.3" rx="3" fill="var(--brass)"><title>Dec: 28% occupancy</title></rect><text x="638.6" y="194.7" font-family="DM Sans,sans-serif" font-size="10" fill="#5a5346" text-anchor="middle">28%</text><text x="638.6" y="284.0" font-family="DM Sans,sans-serif" font-size="10" fill="#8a8276" text-anchor="middle">Dec</text></g></svg><table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin-top:.75rem;font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.8rem"><caption style="text-align:left;font-size:.72rem;letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6d675d;margin-bottom:.4rem">Typical short-term rental occupancy by month (illustrative)</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col" style="text-align:left;padding:.3rem .5rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--navy)">Month</th><th scope="col" style="text-align:right;padding:.3rem .5rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--navy)">Occupancy</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">Jan</td><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;text-align:right;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">24%</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">Feb</td><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;text-align:right;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">28%</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">Mar</td><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;text-align:right;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">38%</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">Apr</td><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;text-align:right;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">50%</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">May</td><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;text-align:right;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">58%</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">Jun</td><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;text-align:right;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">68%</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">Jul</td><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;text-align:right;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">78%</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">Aug</td><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;text-align:right;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">74%</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">Sep</td><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;text-align:right;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">54%</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">Oct</td><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;text-align:right;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">53%</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">Nov</td><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;text-align:right;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">36%</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">Dec</td><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;text-align:right;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">28%</td></tr></tbody></table><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.74rem;color:#6d675d;line-height:1.5;margin-top:.5rem">Illustrative Grand Strand short-term rental seasonality. Little River leans long-term, with selective short-term demand for waterway and boat-access homes.</p></figure><div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid var(--rule);border-radius:8px;padding:1.5rem 1.6rem;margin:2rem 0"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.62rem;letter-spacing:.18em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:1rem">Short-term rental data &middot; Little River</p><div style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(112px,1fr));gap:1.2rem 1rem;margin-bottom:1.15rem"><div><div style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.55rem;color:var(--navy);line-height:1.1">$12,243</div><div style="font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.05em;color:var(--muted);margin-top:.15rem">average annual revenue</div></div><div><div style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.55rem;color:var(--navy);line-height:1.1">30.3%</div><div style="font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.05em;color:var(--muted);margin-top:.15rem">occupancy</div></div><div><div style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.55rem;color:var(--navy);line-height:1.1">$202</div><div style="font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.05em;color:var(--muted);margin-top:.15rem">average nightly rate</div></div><div><div style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.55rem;color:var(--navy);line-height:1.1">171</div><div style="font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.05em;color:var(--muted);margin-top:.15rem">active listings</div></div></div><p style="color:var(--muted);font-size:.87rem;line-height:1.65;margin:0">The strongest tenth of listings here gross $4,106 a month or more; the median listing makes $1,505. June is the strongest month and January the weakest. Averages cover every listing in the market, including part-time ones, so a well-run property can sit well above them. <a href="/invest/airbnb-income/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">Compare all nine submarkets</a>. <span style="font-size:.78rem">AirROI, data through 2026-08-08.</span></p></div><h2>How rental returns work in Little River</h2><p>Little River rewards straightforward long-term-rental math. Lower entry pricing against steady local rents produces some of the cleaner cash-on-cash numbers on the Strand, especially in the newer inland subdivisions. Subtract the standard 35 to 45 percent expense load from achievable rent and the deals often pencil without aggressive assumptions. Waterway homes shift the model toward premium rents and occasional short-term demand, at a higher entry.</p><h2>Financing an investment property in Little River</h2><p>Financing here is mostly straightforward, with one wrinkle: some inventory is manufactured or modular housing, which carries different loan products, terms, and resale behavior than stick-built homes. Know which you are buying. For conventional single-family rentals, standard investment and DSCR loans fit well, and the lower price points keep down-payment requirements manageable.</p><h2>Little River rules and costs that move the numbers</h2><p>Keep the cost side honest. South Carolina assesses non-owner-occupied property at a 6 percent ratio against 4 percent for a primary residence, so an investment rental carries a higher tax line. Confirm the current figure for the parcel. Manufactured homes can be taxed and titled differently, another reason to know exactly what you are buying. Waterway properties may need flood coverage. Little River's lower entry prices keep these absolute costs manageable relative to the rents they support.</p><h2>The Little River investor thesis</h2><ol class="thesis-list"><li><span class="thesis-num">01</span><span class="thesis-text">Lower entry pricing plus genuine waterfront makes Little River a value-and-growth play on the northern Strand.</span></li><li><span class="thesis-num">02</span><span class="thesis-text">Long-term rental cash flow is the steady core, supported by a growing year-round population.</span></li><li><span class="thesis-num">03</span><span class="thesis-text">Waterway and boat-access homes are a premium niche tied to the marinas and fishing economy.</span></li><li><span class="thesis-num">04</span><span class="thesis-text">Buying ahead of the north end's continued growth can reward patient, early investors.</span></li></ol><h2>What a first Little River deal looks like</h2><p>A straightforward first Little River deal is a newer single-family home in an inland subdivision, held as a long-term rental. Lower entry pricing against steady local rents tends to pencil without aggressive assumptions, which is what makes the northern Strand attractive to first-time investors. Chapter3 will confirm whether the home is stick-built or manufactured, since that changes financing and resale, and if a waterway property tempts you, we will verify the dock rights and water depth that justify the premium before you pay it.</p><h2>What to watch before you buy in Little River</h2><ul style="padding-left:1.1rem;margin-top:.5rem"><li style="margin-bottom:.7rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7"><strong>Manufactured and modular financing.</strong> Some inventory is manufactured housing, which carries different financing and resale behavior. Know the product before you buy.</li><li style="margin-bottom:.7rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7"><strong>Waterway access specifics.</strong> Verify dock rights, depth, and waterway access. They drive the premium.</li><li style="margin-bottom:.7rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7"><strong>Still-maturing market.</strong> Growth is a thesis, not a guarantee. Estimate on today's rents, not tomorrow's hopes.</li></ul><h2>Where Little River sits</h2><p>Little River is an unincorporated Horry County community at the far north end of the Grand Strand, right at the South Carolina and North Carolina line near Calabash. It keeps a working-waterfront, fishing-village character along the Intracoastal Waterway, and it sits only about 10 minutes from Cherry Grove and North Myrtle Beach, so buyers get quiet and water access without oceanfront prices.</p><h2>The Little River market right now</h2><p>Little River is more affordable than the oceanfront towns and currently favors buyers. Redfin reported a median near $292,000 for the three months ending May 2026, down about 8 percent year over year, with days on market around 122. Zillow's home-value index was near $321,000 and price per square foot around $197. See the <a href="https://www.redfin.com/city/23919/SC/Little-River/housing-market" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Redfin Little River market page</a>. Inventory includes waterfront and golf-community homes plus gated Intracoastal communities such as Windjammer Village. <!-- TIMMY: current CCAR MLS median, DOM, and the best-value ICW and golf communities. --></p><h2>Short-term rental rules in Little River</h2><p>Little River is unincorporated, so short-term rentals follow Horry County rules rather than a city ordinance, plus any HOA restrictions. Little River is less of a pure nightly-rental market than the oceanfront towns and more of a primary-residence, second-home, and Intracoastal-boating market. Waterfront and near-beach properties can still rent well. Chapter3 will verify the county zoning and pull the HOA governing documents for a specific address before you assume STR income. <!-- TIMMY: which Little River communities allow or restrict rentals, and realistic STR vs LTR performance. --> Every jurisdiction's licenses and taxes: <a href="/invest/str-rules/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">short-term rental rules by city</a>.</p><h2>Owning in Little River: the 4 percent versus 6 percent property tax rule</h2><p>South Carolina assesses an owner-occupied primary residence at a 4 percent ratio, and it does not pay the school operating tax. A second home or investment is assessed at 6 percent and pays the school operating tax. In Little River that makes a second home's tax bill about 3.3 times a primary home's on the same house. See the <a href="https://dor.sc.gov" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">South Carolina Department of Revenue</a> for the assessment-ratio rules. Full county math, with a calculator: <a href="/buyers/property-taxes/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">Horry County property taxes</a>.</p><h2>Flood zones and insurance in Little River</h2><p>Little River's appeal is the water, which also drives its flood profile. Properties along the Intracoastal Waterway and in low-lying areas carry meaningful flood-insurance costs, and public First Street data reported by Redfin flags roughly 23 percent of Little River properties as facing severe flood risk over 30 years. Elevated and newer raised-slab homes generally price better on insurance. Pull the FEMA flood map for the exact address. <!-- TIMMY: which streets/sections flood, and current premium ranges. --> What the three coastal policies cost: <a href="/buyers/coastal-insurance/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">coastal home insurance costs</a>.</p><h2>Little River lifestyle and amenities</h2><p>Little River is built around its Intracoastal waterfront: family-owned seafood restaurants on the docks, a network of marinas, offshore fishing and Calabash dinner cruises, casino boats, and the annual Blue Crab Festival. Vereen Memorial Gardens offers marsh boardwalks and trails, healthcare comes from McLeod Seacoast and Conway Medical Center, and the full range of North Strand shopping and beaches is minutes away.</p><h2>What is coming in Little River</h2><p>Little River is the Grand Strand community most affected by the proposed Carolina Bays Parkway (S.C. 31) extension, which would run north from S.C. 9 toward U.S. 17 near the North Carolina line as a future Interstate 74 corridor. The South Carolina portion is funded through Horry County's RIDE III program with construction targeted around 2029, per <a href="https://myrtlebeachsc.com/carolina-bays-parkway-extension-ncdot-route/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SCDOT statements reported locally</a>, while the North Carolina segment remains unfunded. Improved connectivity toward Wilmington is a long-term value driver worth watching here. <!-- TIMMY: local subdivisions, commercial growth, and 17/31 corridor projects specific to Little River. --></p><h2>Schools serving Little River</h2><p>Public schools fall within <a href="https://www.horrycountyschools.net/about-hcs/our-schools" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Horry County Schools</a>, with area elementary and middle options nearby and high-school students generally attending North Myrtle Beach High School. Assignment is by address, and Chapter3 will confirm the exact schools on the district boundary map.</p><h2>What it is like to live in Little River</h2><p>Little River keeps a working-waterfront, fishing-village character along the Intracoastal Waterway: family-owned seafood restaurants on the docks, marinas, offshore fishing and Calabash dinner cruises, and the annual Blue Crab Festival. It is quiet and small-town, about ten minutes from Cherry Grove and North Myrtle Beach, so residents get water access and beach proximity without oceanfront prices.</p><p>For a live-in buyer, Little River is more affordable than the oceanfront towns, and the appeal is the water. Intracoastal and low-lying lots carry meaningful flood-insurance costs, and Chapter3 will check the FEMA map for the exact address. McLeod Seacoast and Conway Medical Center provide healthcare.</p><h2>Buying in Little River as a primary residence</h2><p>Little River is more a primary-residence and second-home market than a nightly-rental market, which suits buyers who want a quieter, boat-friendly base near the beach. Gated Intracoastal communities like Windjammer Village and nearby golf communities are common. A primary residence is taxed at 4% with the owner-occupant credit; a second home at 6% without it.</p><h2>Read the Little River HOA rules before you commit to a house</h2><p>In a market with this many associations, the covenants matter as much as the house. Before you make an offer, read the HOA bylaws and rules, because they can restrict short-term and long-term rentals, pets, parking, golf carts, fences, and exterior changes. The classic trap: you have spent years training your dog to walk off leash, then buy into a community with a strict leash rule and lose the thing you valued most. Read the documents before you make an offer, not after.</p><p>Ask us and we will get you the governing documents for any Little River community, from our own files if we already have them or by requesting them for you if we do not. It is also why a Chapter3 agent asks a lot of questions about how you live. We would rather spend the time up front than match you to a home with a rule or an HOA that blocks what you enjoy or turns out to be a poor investment.</p><h2>Little River community stats</h2><p>Official figures from government sources, with no interpretation added. Each links to its source so you can read the full data yourself.</p><div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid var(--rule);border-radius:12px;padding:1.25rem 1.4rem;margin:1rem 0"><p style="font-size:.72rem;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:.5rem">Population &amp; income &middot; <a href="https://www.census.gov/quickfacts/fact/table/littlerivercdpsouthcarolina" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">U.S. Census Bureau</a></p><div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;gap:1rem;padding:.55rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.06)"><span style="color:var(--navy)">Population (2020 Census)</span><span style="color:var(--muted);text-align:right">9,692</span></div><div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;gap:1rem;padding:.55rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.06)"><span style="color:var(--navy)">Median household income (ACS)</span><span style="color:var(--muted);text-align:right">about $65,786</span></div><div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;gap:1rem;padding:.55rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.06)"><span style="color:var(--navy)">Persons in poverty (ACS)</span><span style="color:var(--muted);text-align:right">about 10 to 13%</span></div><p style="font-size:.72rem;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;margin:1.25rem 0 .5rem">Cost, taxes &amp; risk &middot; government sources</p><div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;gap:1rem;padding:.55rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.06)"><span style="color:var(--navy)">Median home value</span><span style="color:var(--muted);text-align:right"><a href="https://www.census.gov/quickfacts/fact/table/littlerivercdpsouthcarolina" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">U.S. Census Bureau</a></span></div><div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;gap:1rem;padding:.55rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.06)"><span style="color:var(--navy)">Property assessment ratio</span><span style="color:var(--muted);text-align:right">4% primary / 6% second home &nbsp;<a href="https://dor.sc.gov" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC DOR</a></span></div><div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;gap:1rem;padding:.55rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.06)"><span style="color:var(--navy)">Flood hazard by address</span><span style="color:var(--muted);text-align:right"><a href="https://msc.fema.gov" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">FEMA Flood Map Service Center</a></span></div><div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;gap:1rem;padding:.55rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.06)"><span style="color:var(--navy)">Crime statistics</span><span style="color:var(--muted);text-align:right"><a href="https://cde.ucr.cjis.gov" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">FBI Crime Data Explorer</a> &middot; <a href="https://www.sled.sc.gov" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SLED</a></span></div><div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;gap:1rem;padding:.55rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.06)"><span style="color:var(--navy)">School performance</span><span style="color:var(--muted);text-align:right"><a href="https://screportcards.ed.sc.gov" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC Dept. of Education report cards</a></span></div></div><p style="color:var(--muted);font-size:.85rem;line-height:1.7">How to pull the crime numbers yourself: on the <a href="https://cde.ucr.cjis.gov" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">FBI Crime Data Explorer</a>, choose South Carolina, then select the local agency to see reported offenses by year. The local agency is the county sheriff's office or the city police department. On <a href="https://www.sled.sc.gov" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SLED</a>, open the current "Crime in South Carolina" report and find the county and agency tables. Chapter3 will pull the current figures for a specific address with you.</p><h2>Little River school stats</h2><p>Little River is served by Horry County Schools, with high-school students generally attending North Myrtle Beach High School. Figures are from the South Carolina Department of Education report card and federal education data, with no interpretation added.</p><div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid var(--rule);border-radius:12px;padding:1.25rem 1.4rem;margin:1rem 0"><p style="font-size:.72rem;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:.5rem">North Myrtle Beach High School &middot; <a href="https://screportcards.ed.sc.gov" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC report card</a></p><div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;gap:1rem;padding:.55rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.06)"><span style="color:var(--navy)">Grades served</span><span style="color:var(--muted);text-align:right">9-12</span></div><div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;gap:1rem;padding:.55rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.06)"><span style="color:var(--navy)">Enrollment</span><span style="color:var(--muted);text-align:right">about 1,491</span></div><div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;gap:1rem;padding:.55rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.06)"><span style="color:var(--navy)">State report card rating</span><span style="color:var(--muted);text-align:right">Substantially exceeds expectations</span></div><div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;gap:1rem;padding:.55rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.06)"><span style="color:var(--navy)">Four-year graduation rate</span><span style="color:var(--muted);text-align:right">about 83% (state average about 84%)</span></div><div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;gap:1rem;padding:.55rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.06)"><span style="color:var(--navy)">Math proficiency</span><span style="color:var(--muted);text-align:right">71% (state average 41%)</span></div><div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;gap:1rem;padding:.55rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.06)"><span style="color:var(--navy)">Reading and writing proficiency</span><span style="color:var(--muted);text-align:right">89% (state average 52%)</span></div><div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;gap:1rem;padding:.55rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.06)"><span style="color:var(--navy)">Student-to-teacher ratio</span><span style="color:var(--muted);text-align:right">18 to 1</span></div></div><p>For the elementary and middle schools that feed it, and for any exact address, read the full report cards at the <a href="https://screportcards.ed.sc.gov" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">South Carolina Department of Education</a>. The report card also publishes SAT and ACT averages, Advanced Placement and dual-enrollment participation, college-and-career readiness, dropout rate, and chronic absenteeism. Ask your Chapter3 agent and we will pull the report card for the school an address is zoned to.</p><h2>Who Little River suits</h2><p>Little River fits boaters and Intracoastal buyers, second-home owners who want water access without oceanfront prices, and buyers who prefer a quieter, small-town pace with the beach a short drive away. It is more of a buy-and-hold, long-term-rental, and lifestyle market than a high-season nightly-rental play, so investors chasing peak summer STR income will usually pencil better on the oceanfront.</p></div>
